{"id":"u-01b28cb2-e044-4bff-9357-aa94cc83d0e8","src":"community","title":"Lyle McDonald's generic bulking routine","author":"Dominus","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/noURdb-lyle-mcdonald-generic-bulking-routine","desc":"This routine is based on Lyle McDonald's generic bulking routine from 2014 (including later additions from his blog), and is for people mainly focused on muscle growth, with strength as a secondary goal. The key is to gradually increase the weight while using good, standardized form for moderate to high repetitions.\n\n**Upper-body days** train the chest, back, shoulders, biceps, and triceps. Biceps and triceps need less direct work because chest and back exercises also train them. \n\n**Lower-body days** train the quads, hamstrings, glutes, and calves.\n\n**Progression:** Beginners and early intermediates, aim to improve each time you repeat a workout. If progress stalls, check your effort, food, and rest before changing the routine. For intermediates, you already know the drill, progress will be very very slow.\n\nUse double progression: add reps until you reach the top of the rep range, then increase the weight. \n\nFor example:\n\nWorkout 1: 60 kg × 8, 7, 6, 6\nWorkout 2: 60 kg × 8, 7, 7, 6\nWorkout 3: 60 kg × 8, 8, 7, 6\nWorkout 4: 60 kg × 8, 8, 7, 7\nWorkout 5: 60 kg × 8, 8, 8, 7\nWorkout 6: 60 kg × 8, 8, 8, 8\nWorkout 7: Increase the weight, then work back up from 6 to 8 reps.\n\nThis routine can be run back to back indefinitely. If there's an exercise that doesn't agree with you, swap it for a similar movement: barbell squat to hack squat, bench press to weighted dips etc.","level":["Beginner","Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":80,"joins":65,"rate":5,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-11-03T09:20:19.848Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["De-load","De-load","Day 3","Day 4"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":8,"x":4}],"rest":180},{"n":"Chest Supported Row (